\babel@toc {english}{}\relax 
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Spatial distribution of the 397 charging stations across the Würzburg urban service area, with the central depot marked.\relax }}{4}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Two initial plan strategies for the same set of stations. Left: routing-efficiency approach visits A second despite low urgency. Right: maintenance-urgency approach defers A to last, ensuring critical stations are covered first.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Two replanning decisions under the same incoming disruption E. Left: dropping B (largest detour) leaves the most critical station unserviced. Right: dropping A (recently serviced, low power) allows B to be serviced and averts failure risk.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Hierarchy of decision policies developed in this thesis. All four base policies share the same zone-selection layer (Section~\ref {subsec_shared_zone_selection_logic}) and differ only in their routing score and drop score, shown in each box. The VFA layer (Section~\ref {subsec_value_function_approximation}) is not a fifth base policy but an optional rollout wrapper that can be placed on top of any of the four.\relax }}{29}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Execution timeline of a single simulated day for both teams (CFA, value-based zone selection, seed~1, day~1), reconstructed from the actual simulation log. Both teams start their routine route at 8:00; disruptions (Type~1 and Type~2) are inserted mid-route via cheapest insertion, and the shared 60-minute lunch break is fixed at 12:00--13:00 and interrupts the active service stop if one is ongoing. Of the 20 routine tasks planned for the day, 10 could not be completed and were carried over to day~2.\relax }}{38}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Three-phase benchmark for the number of zones $k$: mean CFA total cost by zone count. Each point represents the average over 100~simulation runs, and colours indicate the benchmark phase.\relax }}{40}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Mean service day per zone for CFA under centrality-based (left) and value-based (right) zone selection, averaged over 500 runs. Green = serviced early, red = serviced late. Under centrality, the service order is strictly concentric, with centrally located zones always visited first and peripheral zones last. Value-based selection breaks this pattern, pulling economically critical zones forward to early service days regardless of their position in the network.\relax }}{43}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Total cost distributions for all four policies under centrality (light) and value-based (dark) zone selection (500 runs each). Boxes show interquartile range, and whiskers extend to 1.5$\times $IQR.\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Cumulative costs (top) and disruptions resolved on the same day (bottom) for Myopic and CFA under value-based zone selection, seed~497 (single run). The dashed vertical line marks day~9, where the gap widens most sharply. Myopic total: 52{,}491~€; CFA total: 37{,}191~€.\relax }}{45}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Distribution of paired cost differences (base minus rollout) per run for each policy, shown as histograms with 250\,€ bins. Positive values indicate the rollout lowered cost, and negative values indicate the rollout increased cost. The dashed vertical line marks zero. Runs with $\Delta = 0$ (no override was triggered) are excluded from the histogram and reported separately in the annotation. The x-axis is clipped to $\pm $4{,}000\,€, and a small number of extreme values fall outside the plotted range. All variants use value-based zone selection (500 runs each).\relax }}{48}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Distribution of replan overrides per run for all four rollout variants (500 runs each). Dashed vertical line shows the mean.\relax }}{49}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Cohen's $d$ for all key comparisons. Dashed vertical lines mark conventional thresholds (small: 0.20, medium: 0.50, large: 0.80). Zone-selection effects are the only comparisons with a large effect size, and all other comparisons fall below the medium threshold ($d = 0.50$).\relax }}{53}{figure.caption.18}%
